#: Darwin `slideTypes.ts: SCAFFOLDS`: slide type -> layout directive.
SCAFFOLDS: dict[str, str] = {
    'title': 'a title slide — full-bleed cover (brand field or dimmed photo) with one large left-aligned headline block of client name, bold deliverable title, accent-underlined subtitle, and date, with logo co-branding pinned to a corner; generous whitespace and nothing else competing.',
    'agenda': 'an agenda slide — a numbered contents list with oversized section numerals beside a dark image or solid dark side panel, reused through the deck as a tracker with a single soft pill highlight on the active section and everything else dimmed to whitespace.',
    'framework': 'a framework slide — one dominant strategy framework (value chain, pyramid, 2x2, hub-and-spoke, layered bands, funnel, cascade) structuring the whole message, with icon-anchored one-to-three-word labels, bolded lead phrases carrying the bullets, and a single accent color or dashed callout reserved for the takeaway; the default body slide.',
    'data': 'a data slide — ONE hero chart (waterfall, 100% stacked bar, annotated line) with data labels on every mark and flags or logos anchoring the categories, the quantified takeaway stated in an action title or banner directly above the chart, and context or implications pushed to side rails so the plot stays clean.',
    'process': 'a process slide — a left-to-right sequence of numbered or lettered steps (chevrons, step columns, swimlanes) with oversized numerals, verb-led headers, and a graduated color ramp encoding progression; each step holds one icon and a tight parallel bullet stack, with deliverables or examples aligned beneath on a shared baseline.',
    'quote': 'a quote slide — one large attributed statement (or a short stack of two or three) with an oversized quotation mark, a photo or logo source tile establishing credibility, and bold reserved for only the operative phrases; whitespace does the framing.',
    'closing': "a closing slide — the recommendation restated as a visual therefore: summary columns joined by plus signs and an arrow resolving into one accent-colored verdict band in large type, with next steps as a short icon-led list; the deck's final page may reduce to a centered logo on a full-bleed brand field.",
    'comparison': "a comparison slide — two or more options or states side-by-side in strictly parallel columns (matched row labels, mirrored bullets, mini schematics or example logos per column), often ordered along a labeled spectrum axis, with green-plus/red-minus rows and the winning column called out by an accent border, 'Recommended' flag, or verdict band.",
    'timeline': 'a timeline slide — milestones on a horizontal calendar-time axis: either a gantt with a left activity rail, alternating month shading, and every bar terminating in a labeled milestone diamond naming the concrete deliverable, or dated milestone callouts alternating above and below a single arrow.',
    'kpi': 'a kpi slide — a dashboard of 3-5 headline metric tiles, each an oversized accent-colored stat (61.5%, +25%, 250+) over a one-line qualifier, set in parallel panels so the numbers land before any prose.',
    'market': 'a market slide — TAM/SAM/SOM sizing as concentric circles or a narrowing funnel with the counts printed inside the segments and the filter criteria labeled at each stage, so the narrowing logic and the resulting number read in one pass.',
    'org': 'an org slide — an organization or governance hierarchy with a left rail naming the tiers (priority setting / coordination / execution) or a level axis (L0-L4), boxes color-coded by function, arrows annotated with what flows between them, and a compact legend; responsibility grids run one owner per row with numbered ID chips.',
    'divider': 'a divider slide — a near-empty section break: a big section number and short heading on a dark or split panel, with at most a ghosted contents list locating the section; whitespace is the composition.',
    'table': 'a table slide — ONE dense matrix as the hero, tamed by structure rather than labels: a fixed row spine with icon-coded group bands and numbered ID chips, cells reduced to checkmarks, harvey balls, dots, or heat shading decoded by a single compact footer legend, and a verdict or takeaway column closing every row.',
    'case-study': 'a case-study slide — one named real-world subject profiled per page: a logo, flag, or photo identity panel anchors the left or top, the body repeats a fixed panel scaffold (overview, source artifact or screenshot exhibit, headline stats), and a dark best-practices sidebar isolates the so-what; sibling slides reuse the template verbatim so the series compares page over page.',
    'charter': 'a charter slide — a one-pager card for a single initiative or governing body: a fixed metadata strip (owner, budget, start date, duration) with small line icons tops a three-column grid of labeled field panels (description, stakeholders, risks, outputs, dependencies), each capped at two or three dash bullets; identical grid positions across instances let readers compare field-by-field.',
    'synthesis': 'a synthesis slide — evidence flowing into conclusions across a two-panel left-to-right layout: a locator rail or framework chips pin the context, a best-practices or findings panel feeds a numbered lessons-and-recommendations panel through a single chevron connector, and every item opens on a bolded lead-in phrase.',
    'navigator': "a navigator slide — the deck's master framework map redrawn as wayfinding: every tile ghosted grey except the active section, which gets a dashed accent outline and a 'Focus of this section' tag; near-zero text, orientation readable in under a second.",
    'benchmark': 'a benchmark slide — peer evidence in logo-anchored rows: each reference organization gets a logo/flag anchor and one verbatim mission sentence or hard-count fact line with theme keywords inline-highlighted in legend colors so the cross-cutting pattern is visible without reading, and a right-rail takeaway panel converts the evidence into design guidelines.',
    'scorecard': "a scorecard slide — one option or candidate evaluated per page against a fixed criteria scaffold: criteria columns or gated question rows scored with harvey balls, checks, or rank badges plus a one-line rationale each, and a single verdict badge ('1st', 'Recommended', or a red X) delivering the call; the scaffold repeats unchanged across sibling options so readers diff on the glyphs alone.",
    'deep-dive': 'a deep-dive slide — a drill-down located by breadcrumb: the parent framework or process repeats in miniature with non-focal stages ghosted grey and only the spotlit element lit, while the body expands that element into fixed detail panels (considerations, definition vs concrete example, photo cards) with identical anatomy across the series.',
    'map': 'a map slide — an annotated geographic map as the hero, with numbered pins or hub-to-spoke arrows placed on the geography and peripheral callout cards (dark name band plus one-line descriptor) tied to each location, a small legend, and the so-what carried in a side rail or banner.',
}

STYLE_GUARDRAILS = 'Create the slide as a single polished image in a strict 16:9 aspect ratio. The output must look like a professional presentation slide, not a website, dashboard, HTML layout, app screen, or web-based design. Use the image model to generate the slide directly; do not use HTML, CSS, code, or browser-style UI elements. Build the slide around one dominant visual framework only; do not combine multiple competing frameworks on the same slide, and do not overcrowd the slide with text that becomes unreadably small. Write the headline as a conclusion-oriented statement that communicates the key strategic message. Do not include a subtitle. Do not render page numbers. Render every layout element — header bands, body panels, callout boxes, data containers, dividers, and connectors — as discrete, flat Microsoft PowerPoint-style shapes drawn ONLY from this vocabulary: rectangles, rounded rectangles, ovals/circles, chevrons, diamonds, trapezoids, parallelograms, pentagons, hexagons, octagons, plus signs, 5- or 6-point stars, block arrows (left/right/up/down), Harvey balls, straight lines, bordered text boxes, icons, and standard data charts (bar, column, line, area, pie, waterfall). Each element must look like an individually placed PPT shape on a slide canvas; do not paint, blend, or merge shapes together into a single illustration. Every arrow, connector, divider, and leader line must be perfectly straight or built from straight segments — never curved, arced, swooshing, or S-shaped; only data series plotted inside a chart may curve. Never draw curly braces or bracket shapes to group items; group with panels, bands, column alignment, or labeled rails instead. Fill every shape with one flat solid color, or leave it unfilled with a plain solid or dashed outline — no gradient fills, no translucent or semi-transparent fills, no shadows, no glows. Render only flat, native-presentation visuals; ignore any instruction elsewhere in this prompt requesting 3D, isometric, photorealistic, or web-style visualizations, gradient or translucent fills, curved arrows or swooshes, or brace/bracket grouping shapes.'

CONTENT_TILE_GUARDRAILS = "Render ONLY the slide BODY content — bullets, panels, callouts, charts, and supporting visuals — as a tile that will be composited onto a branded slide template. Do NOT render the slide title or headline anywhere in this image: the title is placed separately as native text in the template's title placeholder, so drawing it here would duplicate it. The background MUST be plain solid white: no other background color, gradient, pattern, or checkerboard behind the content. Do NOT draw any logo, footer, page number, slide-edge border, or page chrome — none of that furniture belongs in this tile; it comes from the template. Fill the entire image edge to edge with the body content and leave NO outer margin — the content region IS the whole image. The output must look like flat, native Microsoft PowerPoint shapes, not a website, dashboard, HTML layout, app screen, or web-based design. Use the image model to generate the content directly; do not use HTML, CSS, code, or browser-style UI elements. Build the content around one dominant visual framework only; do not combine multiple competing frameworks, and do not overcrowd it with text that becomes unreadably small. Render every layout element — header bands, body panels, callout boxes, data containers, dividers, and connectors — as discrete, flat Microsoft PowerPoint-style shapes drawn ONLY from this vocabulary: rectangles, rounded rectangles, ovals/circles, chevrons, diamonds, trapezoids, parallelograms, pentagons, hexagons, octagons, plus signs, 5- or 6-point stars, block arrows (left/right/up/down), Harvey balls, straight lines, bordered text boxes, icons, and standard data charts (bar, column, line, area, pie, waterfall). Each element must look like an individually placed PPT shape on a slide canvas; do not paint, blend, or merge shapes together into a single illustration. Every arrow, connector, divider, and leader line must be perfectly straight or built from straight segments — never curved, arced, swooshing, or S-shaped; only data series plotted inside a chart may curve. Never draw curly braces or bracket shapes to group items; group with panels, bands, column alignment, or labeled rails instead. Fill every shape with one flat solid color, or leave it unfilled with a plain solid or dashed outline — no gradient fills, no translucent or semi-transparent fills, no shadows, no glows. Render only flat, native-presentation visuals; ignore any instruction elsewhere in this prompt requesting 3D, isometric, photorealistic, or web-style visualizations, gradient or translucent fills, curved arrows or swooshes, or brace/bracket grouping shapes."

RTL_STYLE_OVERRIDE = 'ARABIC / RIGHT-TO-LEFT SLIDE — these rules take ABSOLUTE PRECEDENCE over every layout, framework, and style instruction above. Any instruction above that says "left", "right", "left-to-right", "leading", "first column", "sidebar", or "rail" was written for a left-to-right slide; reinterpret each of them as its right-to-left MIRROR. The whole slide is mirrored horizontally: reading starts at the TOP-RIGHT and flows leftward and down. Right-align the action title, all body text, bullets, list markers, and any source/footnote line; indentation is on the right. Put primary sidebars, rails, category/row labels, and the FIRST item of any sequence on the RIGHT, with the sequence progressing toward the LEFT. EVERY directional element runs right-to-left: arrows and arrowheads point LEFT; process chevrons and step banners point LEFT and are ordered step 1 on the right to the last step on the left; funnels narrow toward the LEFT; timelines and Gantt bars run earliest on the RIGHT to latest on the LEFT; growth/trend lines rise toward the LEFT; connectors and "leads-to" arrows flow LEFT; and any result / outcome / "therefore" box sits at the far LEFT (the end of the flow). Do NOT merely reposition these shapes — ROTATE or FLIP the arrowheads and chevron points themselves so they physically face LEFT. Keep step and sequence numbers consecutive and correct (1, 2, 3, …) with number 1 on the RIGHT; never skip, drop, or reorder a number. Render Arabic as fully joined, flowing cursive script with correct contextual letterforms (isolated, initial, medial, final) and standard ligatures, and SPELL every word correctly; do not disconnect letters, do not add letter-spacing between Arabic letters, and do not render broken or placeholder (tofu) glyphs. Give Arabic generous line-height so ascenders and descenders do not collide. Mirror charts and tables: reverse the category (X) axis so it runs right-to-left AND reverse the series order to match (a time axis runs earliest on the right, latest on the left); order table columns right-to-left so the first/label column is on the right. Numbers and data labels stay left-to-right within their own box. Keep every number, percentage, currency amount, range, date, unit, and Latin brand or product name left-to-right and in its normal order — each is a left-to-right island inside the right-to-left text; never reverse or mirror them, and keep %, $, +, - and other symbols on their correct side. Use Western/Latin digits (0-9). Any embedded label, caption, axis title, legend, or placeholder text must be written in Arabic — do NOT leave English boilerplate (e.g. "TIMELINE", "OVERVIEW", "Days") inside an otherwise-Arabic slide. Reposition brand furniture to the mirrored side, but do NOT horizontally flip or mirror logos, photographs, portraits, or symmetric icons — flipping reverses embedded branding into gibberish. Mirror ONLY meaning-bearing directional graphics (arrows, chevrons, funnels, timelines, progress bars, trend lines, process flows).'

RTL_LAYOUT_MIRROR_NOTE = ' [RIGHT-TO-LEFT: the layout/framework just described is written for a left-to-right slide — MIRROR it for this Arabic slide: swap every left/right (sidebars, rails, columns, labels), order all sequences and steps from right (first) to left (last), and make every arrow, arrowhead, chevron, funnel, timeline, and flow point right-to-left.]'

